    "title": "Where to watch the Masters: TV channel, live stream and tee times",
    "text": "AUGUSTA, GEORGIA - APRIL 09: Rory McIlroy of Northern Ireland lines up a putt on the eighth green during the first round of the 2026 Masters Tournament at Augusta National Golf Club on April 09, 2026 in Augusta, Georgia. (Photo by Andrew Redington/Getty Images)Andrew Redington/Getty ImagesWhile it’s still early, defending champion Rory McIlroy has an opportunity to win back-to-back Masters titles, a feat that hasn’t happened since Tiger Woods did it in 2002.McIlroy opened his title defense in impressive fashion, shooting a 5-under 67 to share the lead with Sam Burns.Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢McIlroy overcame some early inconsistency to put together a composed round, while Burns leaned on excellent ball-striking to match him at the top. A group of contenders sits just behind at 3-under, including Patrick Reed, Jason Day, and Kurt Kitayama, all of whom had stretches where they threatened the lead—Reed notably carded two eagles during his round.Several big names are firmly in the mix at 2-under, including world No. 1 Scottie Scheffler, Xander Schauffele, Justin Rose, and Shane Lowry. Scheffler started hot but cooled off as conditions toughened later in the day.Overall, the leaderboard is tightly packed after Day 1, with plenty of star power near the top and a challenging course setting the stage for an intriguing second round.Here's how to keep up with Round 2 of the Masters. Article continues below this adWhat time does the Masters start today? Here's what to know about times for Round 2 of the Masters. All times are Central: Start time: 6:40 a.m. Masters on the Range: 7:30 a.m. to 9:30 a.m Featured Groups: 8:55 a.m. to 6:30 p.m. 8:55 a.m. — Jordan Spieth, Justin Rose, Brooks Koepka 9:19 a.m. — Scottie Scheffler, Robert MacIntyre, Gary Woodland 11:56 a.m. — Dustin Johnson, Shane Lowry, Jason Day 12:44 p.m. — Rory McIlroy, Cameron Young, Mason Howell Article continues below this adMasters 2026 Round 2 tee timesAll times Central 6:40 a.m. – Samuel Stevens, Sungjae Im 6:50 a.m. – Andrew Novak, Tom McKibbin, Brian Campbell 7:02 a.m. – Mike Weir, Wyndham Clark, Mateo Pulcini 7:14 a.m. – Zach Johnson, Michael Kim, Nicolai Hojgaard 7:26 a.m. – Danny Willett, Davis Riley, Ethan Fang 7:38 a.m. – Adam Scott, Daniel Berger, Brian Harman 7:50 a.m. – Fred Couples, Min Woo Lee, Fifa Laopakdee 8:02 a.m. – Sergio Garcia, Aaron Rai, Jacob Bridgeman 8:19 a.m. – Harry Hall, Corey Conners, Michael Brennan 8:31 a.m. – J.J. Spaun, Maverick McNealy, Tyrrell Hatton 8:43 a.m. – Jon Rahm, Chris Gotterup, Ludvig Åberg 8:55 a.m. – Jordan Spieth, Justin Rose, Brooks Koepka 9:07 a.m. – Sepp Straka, Ben Griffin, Justin Thomas 9:19 a.m. – Scottie Scheffler, Robert MacIntyre, Gary Woodland 9:31 a.m. – Harris English, Marco Penge, Si Woo Kim 9:51 a.m. – John Keefer, Haotong Li 10:03 a.m. – Naoyuki Kataoaka, Max Homa, Carlos Ortiz 10:15 a.m. – Jose Maria Olazabal, Rasmus Neegaard-Petersen, Aldrich Potgieter 10:27 a.m. – Angel Cabrera, Sami Valimaki, Jackson Herrington 10:39 a.m. – Charl Schwartzel, Max Greyserman, Ryan Fox 10:51 a.m. – Vijay Singh, Matt McCarty, Rasmus Hojgaard 11:03 a.m. – Kurt Kitayama, Kristofer Reitan, Casey Jarvis 11:15 a.m. – Bubba Watson, Nicolas Echavarria, Brandon Holtz 11:32 a.m. – Cameron Smith, Sam Burns, Jake Knapp 11:44 a.m. – Keegan Bradley, Ryan Gerard, Nick Taylor 11:56 a.m. – Dustin Johnson, Shane Lowry, Jason Day 12:08 p.m. – Patrick Reed, Tommy Fleetwood, Akshay Bhatia 12:20 p.m. – Bryson DeChambeau, Matt Fitzpatrick, Xander Schauffele 12:32 p.m. – Hideki Matsuyama, Collin Morikawa, Russell Henley 12:44 p.m. – Rory McIlroy, Cameron Young, Mason Howell 12:56 p.m. – Viktor Hovland, Patrick Cantlay, Alex Noren Start time: 7:30 a.m. Central TimeTV: CBS, ESPNLive stream: CBSSports.com, CBS Sports app, Paramount+, Masters.com, the Masters app and Prime VideoArticle continues below this adMasters live streaming schedule Here's the streaming schedule for the Masters. All times are Central: Early Round 2 Coverage: 12 p.m. to 2 p.m. (Prime Video) Round 2 Coverage: 2 p.m. to 6:30 p.m. (ESPN, Masters.com) Featured Groups & Holes: 8:15 a.m. to 6:30 p.m. (Masters.com, Paramount+, Prime Video) Article continues below this adHow many people make the cut at the Masters?According to the PGA Tour, at the Masters, only the top 50 players (including ties) after the first 36 holes advance to the weekend rounds. This rule, introduced in 2020, replaced the earlier system that also allowed players within 10 strokes of the lead to make the cut. As a result, the final two rounds usually feature just over 50 competitors.Get the hookup and your Horns Up with Longhorns coverage.April 10, 2026Marley Malenfant is a 10-year multimedia journalist who’s worked in print, digital, and broadcast. His prior work includes working in West Texas as a TV reporter for KTXS. During his time there, he covered education, sports, features, and breaking News. He’s skilled in front of the camera as well as behind it. He was a freelance writer, published in D Magazine, Dallas Morning News, SB Nation, Dallas Observer, and was a personality for “The Coach Brothers” sports radio show. Outside of journalism, he was an educator in the Dallas area. He was a 2012 Mayborn Literary Nonfiction Conference grant recipient. In 2021, he was part of the NABJ student multimedia project. He graduated with a paralegal degree from El Centro in 2023. Currently, he’s a trending news reporter for the Austin American-Statesman, covering breaking News, sports, music, and legal trends.",
